#+TITLE: dovecot-vpopmail — DSMR Package Documentation
#+AUTHOR: Brian O'Reilly <fade@deepsky.com>
#+DATE: 2026-03-22

* Summary

~dovecot-vpopmail~ is a configuration-only package (no compiled binaries) that
wires dovecot 2.4 into the DSMR qmail/vpopmail stack.  It installs conf.d
snippets that authenticate IMAP users against the vpopmail PostgreSQL database
and serve their Maildirs.  TLS is required on all connections; the certificate
is taken from (or shares its origin with) the qmail TLS certificate.

| Field         | Value                                          |
|---------------+------------------------------------------------|
| Source        | dovecot-vpopmail                               |
| Binary        | dovecot-vpopmail                               |
| Version       | 2.4.1-8                                        |
| Architecture  | all (config only)                              |
| Conf snippets | ~/etc/dovecot/conf.d/~                         |
| Generated     | ~/etc/dovecot/dovecot-vpopmail-sql.conf.ext~   |
| TLS cert      | ~/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem~ + ~.key~    |
| Ports         | 143 (IMAP+STARTTLS), 993 (IMAPS)              |
| Target        | Debian 13 Trixie                               |

* Description

Dovecot 2.4 removed the vpopmail passdb and checkpassword passdb drivers that
were present in 2.3.  The only supported path for authenticating vpopmail
virtual users is the SQL passdb driver querying the ~vpopmail~ PostgreSQL table
directly.

This package provides that configuration.  It ships five conf.d snippets and
a postinst that generates the PostgreSQL connection block (with credentials)
from the vpopmail credential file written by the vpopmail package.

** Authentication flow

1. IMAP client connects on port 993 (IMAPS) or 143 (IMAP+STARTTLS).
2. Client sends LOGIN with ~user@domain~ and plaintext password (over TLS).
3. ~05-vpopmail-auth.conf~ loads first (before ~10-auth.conf~), so the SQL
   passdb is tried before the system PAM passdb.
4. dovecot executes the ~password_query~ against the ~vpopmail~ PostgreSQL table.
5. The returned ~pw_passwd~ (~$6$...~ SHA-512 crypt hash) is verified against
   the user-supplied password using ~default_password_scheme = SHA512-CRYPT~.
6. On success the ~user_query~ returns ~pw_dir~ as the home directory.
7. dovecot sets uid/gid to 89 (vpopmail:vchkpw) and opens
   ~<pw_dir>/Maildir/~ as the user's INBOX.

** INBOX and Maildir compatibility

Dovecot 2.4 defaults to storing INBOX at ~<mail_path>/.INBOX/~, which is
incompatible with qmail/vdelivermail which delivers to ~<pw_dir>/Maildir/new/~.
This package sets ~mail_inbox_path = ~/Maildir~ so that dovecot's INBOX IS
the Maildir root, matching qmail delivery.

** Authentication flow — system users

Unix system users (e.g. ~marvin~) can also retrieve mail via IMAP using
~marvin@defaultdomain~ addressing.  The auth chain falls through from the SQL
passdb (user not found in vpopmail table) to ~passdb passwd-file~, which reads
~/etc/dovecot/system-passwd~ (owned ~root:dovecot 640~).

The file uses standard ~passwd(5)~ format.  The password field *must* carry
a ~{CRYPT}~ prefix; dovecot does not auto-detect crypt-style hashes:

#+BEGIN_SRC
marvin:{CRYPT}$6$...:1001:1002::/home/marvin:/bin/bash
#+END_SRC

~auth_username_format = %{user|username}~ strips the domain before the lookup
so ~marvin@example.com~ finds the ~marvin~ entry.

For qmail delivery, the user must also appear in ~/var/lib/qmail/users/assign~
with an exact-match entry using the *virtualdomains-transformed* address (not
the raw ~user@domain~ address):

#+BEGIN_SRC
=example.com-marvin:marvin:1001:1002:/home/marvin:::
#+END_SRC

(~example.com~ is in ~virtualdomains~, so qmail transforms ~marvin@example.com~
to the local address ~example.com-marvin~ before the CDB lookup.)

** Installed conf.d snippets

| File                          | Load order | Purpose                              |
|-------------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------|
| ~05-vpopmail-auth.conf~       | before 10  | ~auth_mechanisms~; include SQL       |
| ~vpopmail-auth-sql.conf.ext~  | via 05     | passdb/userdb: SQL + system-passwd   |
| ~99-vpopmail-mail.conf~       | after all  | Maildir layout, uid/gid, INBOX       |
| ~99-vpopmail-master.conf~     | after all  | auth socket permissions              |
| ~99-vpopmail-ssl.conf~        | after all  | ~ssl = required~                     |

** Generated files (not conffiles)

| File                                         | Owner/Mode       | Purpose                |
|----------------------------------------------+------------------+------------------------|
| ~/etc/dovecot/dovecot-vpopmail-sql.conf.ext~ | root:dovecot 640 | PostgreSQL connection block |
| ~/etc/dovecot/system-passwd~                 | root:dovecot 640 | System user auth file  |
| ~/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem~           | root:dovecot 644 | TLS certificate        |
| ~/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key~           | root:dovecot 600 | TLS private key        |

* TL;DR Runtime Documentation

** Installation

Install in dependency order; vpopmail and its PostgreSQL database must exist first:

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
sudo dpkg -i dovecot-core_*.deb dovecot-imapd_*.deb dovecot-pgsql_*.deb
sudo dpkg -i dovecot-vpopmail_2.4.1-8_all.deb
#+END_SRC

The postinst reads ~/home/vpopmail/etc/vpopmail.pgsql~, generates the dovecot
PostgreSQL connection file, extracts the TLS certificate from
~/etc/qmail/servercert.pem~, and reloads dovecot.

** Service management

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
sudo systemctl start dovecot
sudo systemctl stop dovecot
sudo systemctl restart dovecot
sudo systemctl status dovecot

# Soft reload (re-reads config without disconnecting users)
sudo doveadm reload
#+END_SRC

** Testing IMAP login

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# IMAPS (port 993) — TLS from the start
(echo 'a LOGIN user@example.com password'
 sleep 1
 echo 'b SELECT INBOX'
 sleep 1
 echo 'c LOGOUT') | openssl s_client -connect localhost:993 -quiet 2>&1 \
   | grep -E '^[a-c] |^\* (OK|NO|BYE|EXISTS|UNSEEN)'
#+END_SRC

A successful login shows ~a OK [...] Logged in~ and SELECT INBOX shows
~b OK [READ-WRITE] Select completed~.

** Checking the PostgreSQL connection

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# View generated connection config (root only)
sudo cat /etc/dovecot/dovecot-vpopmail-sql.conf.ext

# Test the SQL query manually
sudo psql -U vpopmail vpopmail \
  -c "SELECT pw_name, pw_domain, pw_dir FROM vpopmail LIMIT 5;"
#+END_SRC

** Regenerating the SQL connection config

If ~/home/vpopmail/etc/vpopmail.pgsql~ is updated (e.g. after rotating
the database password), regenerate the dovecot connection file by
re-running the postinst configure action:

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
sudo rm /etc/dovecot/dovecot-vpopmail-sql.conf.ext
sudo dpkg-reconfigure dovecot-vpopmail
sudo doveadm reload
#+END_SRC

** Replacing the TLS certificate

The postinst only creates the dovecot certificate if it does not already
exist.  To replace it:

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# Option A: re-extract from the qmail combined PEM
sudo rm /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key
sudo openssl x509 -in /etc/qmail/servercert.pem \
  -out /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem
sudo openssl pkey -in /etc/qmail/servercert.pem \
  -out /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key
sudo chmod 644 /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem
sudo chmod 600 /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key
sudo chown root:dovecot /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem \
                        /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key
sudo doveadm reload

# Option B: generate a fresh self-signed certificate
sudo rm /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key
sudo dpkg-reconfigure dovecot-vpopmail
#+END_SRC

** Viewing authentication logs

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# All recent dovecot events
sudo journalctl -u dovecot --no-pager -n 50

# Auth failures only
sudo journalctl -u dovecot --no-pager -n 100 \
  | grep -E 'auth_failed|AUTHENTICATIONFAILED|sql.*mismatch'

# Enable verbose auth logging temporarily
echo 'auth_verbose = yes' | sudo tee /etc/dovecot/conf.d/99-debug.conf
sudo doveadm reload
# … test logins …
sudo rm /etc/dovecot/conf.d/99-debug.conf
sudo doveadm reload
#+END_SRC

** Adding a system user for IMAP access

System users authenticate via ~/etc/dovecot/system-passwd~ (created empty by
postinst, owned ~root:dovecot 640~).  Mail is delivered by qmail using the
~users/assign~ exact-match entry.

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
# 1. Create the Unix user and their Maildir
sudo useradd -m marvin
sudo mkdir -p /home/marvin/Maildir/{new,cur,tmp}
sudo chown -R marvin:marvin /home/marvin/Maildir
echo './Maildir/' | sudo tee /home/marvin/.qmail
sudo chown marvin:marvin /home/marvin/.qmail

# 2. Generate a SHA-512 password hash and add to system-passwd
HASH=$(openssl passwd -6 'thepassword')
echo "marvin:{CRYPT}${HASH}:$(id -u marvin):$(id -g marvin)::/home/marvin:/bin/bash" \
  | sudo tee -a /etc/dovecot/system-passwd
sudo doveadm reload

# 3. Route marvin@example.com to the system user in qmail users/assign
#    (Insert BEFORE the +example.com- wildcard line; exact matches take priority)
sudo sed -i "/^+example.com-/i =example.com-marvin:marvin:$(id -u marvin):$(id -g marvin):/home/marvin:::" \
  /var/lib/qmail/users/assign
sudo /var/lib/qmail/bin/qmail-newu

# 4. Verify
sudo doveadm auth test marvin@example.com thepassword
sudo doveadm user marvin@example.com
#+END_SRC

** Listing all vpopmail IMAP users (doveadm)

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
sudo doveadm user '*'
#+END_SRC

** Checking a user's mailbox

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
sudo doveadm mailbox status -u user@example.com all INBOX
sudo doveadm mailbox list -u user@example.com
sudo doveadm user user@example.com
# Shows: uid, gid, home, mail_path
#+END_SRC

** Rebuilding from source

#+BEGIN_SRC sh
cd dovecot-vpopmail-2.4.1
dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b
#+END_SRC

No build dependencies beyond ~debhelper-compat~; this is a configuration-only
package with no compiled code.
